    "reading": {
        "body": "Amazon's quarterly capital expenditure reached 54.2 billion dollars in the quarter ending June 2026, up from 14.9 billion dollars two years earlier, and it is the largest single-quarter figure among the eight data center builders tracked here.\n\nThat nearly fourfold increase in two years is not a single jump but a climb through every quarter in the series, 14.9 billion, then 17.6 billion, then 22.6 billion, then 25 billion, then 32.2 billion, then 35.1 billion, then 44.2 billion, then 54.2 billion. Each reported quarter exceeds the one before it, which is a different shape than CoreWeave's late spike or Digital Realty's dip and recovery elsewhere in this peer set, and it points to compute and power infrastructure spending that has compounded steadily rather than arriving in a single burst.\n\nAmazon's latest quarter alone exceeds the combined capex of Meta, Oracle, CoreWeave, Equinix, and Digital Realty, the five smallest builders in this set. Two SEC filings in early and mid 2026 record new financial obligations and related agreements, a moderate pace of disclosed financing activity compared to CoreWeave's six filings but well above Microsoft's and Digital Realty's zero.",
